     "content": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nimport re\n\ndef word_count_top(text, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts word frequencies in text, cleans words, and returns the k most frequent.\n\n    Args:\n        text (str): The input text.\n        k (int): The number of top words to return.\n\n    Returns:\n        list: A list of (word, count) tuples, sorted by count (desc) and then word (asc).\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Lowercase the text\n    text = text.lower()\n\n    # 2. Split it on whitespace\n    words = text.split()\n\n    # 3. Strip the characters .,!?;: from both ends of every word\n    cleaned_words = []\n    for word in words:\n        # Strip punctuation from both ends\n        cleaned_word = word.strip('.,!?;:')\n        # 4. Ignore empty strings\n        if cleaned_word:\n            cleaned_words.append(cleaned_word)\n\n    # Count word frequencies\n    word_counts = Counter(cleaned_words)\n\n    # Get the k most common words\n    # most_common returns a list of (element, count) tuples, sorted by count descending.\n    most_common = word_counts.most_common(k)\n\n    # 5. Sort the results:\n    # Primary sort key: count (highest first, which is already handled by most_common)\n    # Secondary sort key: word (alphabetically, which means ascending order for the word)\n    # We use a lambda function for the key: (-count, word)\n    # Since most_common already sorts by count descending, we only need to ensure\n    # the secondary sort is alphabetical (ascending) for ties in count.\n    \n    # The standard way to sort by count (desc) and word (asc) is:\n    # key=lambda item: (-item[1], item[0])\n    \n    sorted_results = sorted(most_common, key=lambda item: (-item[1], item[0]))\n\n    return sorted_results\n```",
